## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical experience with event-driven pipelines, schema evolution, and reliable read-model updates in real-world systems.

## Key Concepts

- Event sourcing, Kafka at-least-once vs exactly-once, Schema Registry compatibility
- Idempotent consumers, replay/backfill strategies, fault injection
- Observability, test data generation, and staged rollouts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test to validate idempotent read-model update
function testIdempotentUpdate(eventStream) {
  const before = readModel.snapshot();
  applyEvents(eventStream);
  const after = readModel.snapshot();
  assert.deepEqual(before, after, "idempotent updates must yield same state on replay");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and handle schema drift in the registry?
- How would you validate backfill correctness when the read model is rebuilt?

## Why This Is Asked

This explores testing for data pipelines at scale with evolving schemas and end-user dashboards, a common real-world constraint for Databricks and MongoDB integrations, while Stripe-like throughput expectations stress latency and correctness.

## Key Concepts

- Streaming CDC and Spark Structured Streaming
- Delta Lake schema evolution and compatibility
- Idempotence and deduplication in CDC pipelines
- Backfill handling and replay safety
- Canary deployments and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: seed events, run streaming, validate dedup and schema
async function testStreamPipeline() {
  const events = generateEvents({n: 100000, schemaVersion: 2, duplicates: true});
  await publishToMongo(events);
  const results = await collectDeltaLakeRows(loopBackoff(60s));
  assert(results.deduplicated === events.length);
  assert(results.latency < 2000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate incompatible schema changes and verify rollback?
- What metrics and tooling would you use to monitor drift and backpressure?
